            ("What basic law of electricity defines the linear relationship between voltage (V), electric current (I), and electrical resistance (R) as V = IR?", "Ohm\'s Law", ["Joule\'s Law", "Kirchhoff\'s Current Law", "Faraday\'s Law"], "Ohm\'s law governs linear electrical circuits: current is directly proportional to voltage and inversely proportional to resistance."),
            ("What is the standard SI unit of electric current, defined by flow of one Coulomb of electrical charge per second?", "Ampere (A)", ["Volt (V)", "Ohm", "Watt (W)"], "Named after Andre-Marie Ampere, the ampere quantifies rate of flow of elementary electrical charges through a conductor."),
            ("What standard SI unit measures electrical potential difference and electromotive force?", "Volt (V)", ["Ampere (A)", "Ohm", "Farad (F)"], "One volt is defined as the electric potential difference between two points that imparts one joule of energy per coulomb of charge."),
            ("What standard SI unit measures electrical resistance in an electric circuit?", "Ohm", ["Siemens", "Henry", "Tesla"], "One ohm of resistance allows one ampere of current to flow under a potential difference of one volt."),
            ("What physical phenomenon describes the creation of an electromotive force (voltage) across an electrical conductor exposed to a changing magnetic field?", "Electromagnetic Induction (Faraday\'s Law)", ["The Hall Effect", "The Photoelectric Effect", "Thermoelectric Effect"], "Discovered by Michael Faraday in 1831, induction forms the operational basis of electric generators, transformers, and induction motors."),
            ("What law states that the direction of an induced electric current always opposes the magnetic flux change that produced it?", "Lenz\'s Law", ["Faraday\'s Law", "Ampere\'s Law", "Biot-Savart Law"], "Lenz\'s law enforces conservation of energy, dictating the negative sign in Faraday\'s mathematical equation."),
            ("What four classical mathematical equations formulated by a Scottish physicist unify electricity, magnetism, and light into classical electromagnetism?", "Maxwell\'s Equations", ["Newton\'s Laws", "Einstein\'s Field Equations", "Schrodinger\'s Equations"], "James Clerk Maxwell demonstrated in 1865 that electric and magnetic fields propagate through empty space as transverse waves at the speed of light."),
            ("What type of wave features particle displacement perpendicular (at right angles) to the direction of wave propagation (e.g., light waves)?", "Transverse Wave", ["Longitudinal Wave", "Compressional Wave", "Standing Wave"], "Electromagnetic waves and shear S-waves are transverse waves that can be physically polarized."),
            ("What type of mechanical wave features particle displacement parallel to the direction of wave energy propagation (e.g., sound waves in air)?", "Longitudinal Wave", ["Transverse Wave", "Surface Wave", "Torsional Wave"], "Sound travels through air via alternating compressions (high density) and rarefactions (low density) along the axis of propagation."),
            ("What is the standard SI unit of wave frequency, measuring the number of complete periodic cycles per second?", "Hertz (Hz)", ["Becquerel", "Radian", "Decibel"], "Named after Heinrich Hertz who proved electromagnetic waves, 1 Hz represents one complete wave oscillation per second."),
            ("What physical wave phenomenon describes the apparent shift in observed frequency and pitch of a wave when source and observer move relative to each other?", "The Doppler Effect", ["Wave Refraction", "Wave Diffraction", "The Compton Effect"], "Approaching sound sources compress waves into higher frequencies (higher pitch); receding sources stretch waves into lower frequencies."),
            ("What optical phenomenon describes the bending of a light wave as it passes at an angle from one optical medium into another with a different refractive index?", "Refraction", ["Reflection", "Diffraction", "Polarization"], "Light changes velocity when entering media of differing optical densities, described mathematically by Snell\'s Law."),
            ("What physical principle states that every point on a propagating wavefront can be considered a source of secondary spherical wavelets?", "Huygens\' Principle", ["Fermat\'s Principle", "Babinet\'s Principle", "Bragg\'s Law"], "Huygens\' construction explains wave diffraction around obstacles and refraction through optical interfaces."),
            ("What optical phenomenon occurs when light propagating in a denser medium strikes an interface at an angle greater than the critical angle, reflecting 100% back into the medium?", "Total Internal Reflection", ["Specular Reflection", "Diffuse Refraction", "Optical Birefringence"], "Total internal reflection confines light pulses inside fiber-optic cables with virtually zero transmission leakage."),
            ("What quantum phenomenon, explained by Albert Einstein in 1905, involves the instantaneous ejection of electrons from a metal surface struck by light photons?", "The Photoelectric Effect", ["The Compton Effect", "The Zeeman Effect", "Thermionic Emission"], "Einstein proved light behaves as quantized discrete energy packets (photons, E = hf), earning the 1921 Nobel Prize in Physics."),
            ("What fundamental quantum principle formulated by Werner Heisenberg states that one cannot simultaneously know with arbitrary precision both the position and momentum of a subatomic particle?", "The Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle", ["The Pauli Exclusion Principle", "The Correspondence Principle", "Bell\'s Theorem"], "The product of position uncertainty (Delta x) and momentum uncertainty (Delta p) must be greater than or equal to h-bar / 2."),
            ("What quantum mechanical rule states that two identical fermions (such as electrons) cannot occupy the exact same quantum state simultaneously within a quantum system?", "The Pauli Exclusion Principle", ["Hund\'s Rule", "The Aufbau Principle", "The Heisenberg Principle"], "Formulated by Wolfgang Pauli, this exclusion explains electron shell configurations and prevents matter from collapsing."),
            ("What quantum concept asserts that all matter and light exhibit behaviors characteristic of both localized particles and continuous waves?", "Wave-Particle Duality", ["Quantum Entanglement", "Quantum Tunneling", "Superposition"], "Louis de Broglie demonstrated in 1924 that moving particles with momentum p possess a matter wavelength equal to h / p."),
            ("What revolutionary 1905 physical equation demonstrates the equivalence and interconvertibility of mass (m) and energy (E)?", "E = mc^2", ["F = ma", "p = mv", "Delta E = hf"], "Einstein\'s mass-energy equivalence dictates that small amounts of mass convert into colossal releases of energy in nuclear reactions."),
            ("What theoretical maximum speed in the universe dictates that no physical mass or information can travel faster than light in a vacuum?", "The Speed of Light (c)", ["Escape Velocity", "The Sound Barrier (Mach 1)", "Terminal Velocity"], "Special Relativity proves that accelerating an object with mass to light speed requires infinite energy, making 'c' the absolute speed limit."),
            ("What general relativity concept explains gravity not as an invisible pull force, but as the curvature of four-dimensional spacetime caused by mass and energy?", "Spacetime Curvature (General Relativity)", ["String Theory", "Newtonian Universal Gravitation", "Loop Quantum Gravity"], "Einstein showed in 1915 that massive celestial objects warp the surrounding fabric of spacetime, dictating the orbital paths of matter and light.")
